## Warm-up {minutes="4"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: your route as arrows; which arrow is the judgment about?

Two or three from the room. Keep one where the attribute is deliberately left out.
:::

You were asked to bring one decision in which an attribute is used, or deliberately not used, with one route by which it could affect the outcome, and whether that route is fair.

::: {.ask}
Draw your route as arrows, from the attribute to the outcome. Which arrow is your judgment about?
:::

# Part 1: The red car

## Pricing without gender

::: {.notes}
Test-Achats, a Belgian consumers' association. The Court struck down the exemption that let member states allow gender-based premiums. The Commission's guidelines: gender may still be used at the aggregate level, not to set an individual's premium.
:::

Court of Justice of the European Union, press release on *Test-Achats*, 1 March 2011:

> "Taking the gender of the insured individual into account as a risk factor in insurance contracts constitutes discrimination"

From 21 December 2012: no differences by gender in individuals' premiums, in new contracts.

::: {.source}
Court of Justice of the European Union (2011), Press Release No 12/11, 1 March 2011, on Case C-236/09, *Association belge des Consommateurs Test-Achats ASBL and Others v Conseil des ministres*, https://curia.europa.eu/site/upload/docs/application/pdf/2011-03/cp110012en.pdf, pages 1--2. European Commission (2012), Guidelines on the application of Council Directive 2004/113/EC to insurance, in the light of the judgment in Case C-236/09 (Test-Achats), *Official Journal of the European Union* C 11, 13 January 2012, 1--11, paragraphs 5 and 14.
:::

## The red car

::: {.notes}
Kusner et al.'s first scenario, with race; ms4ds has it with gender, A = 1 for a woman. Women drive more red cars and are no more aggressive.
:::


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](slides_files/figure-revealjs/dag_redcar-1.png){fig-alt='A causal graph in boxes: A, gender, points into X, car color; U, aggression, points into X and into Y, claims. There is no arrow from A to U or from A to Y.' width=100%}
:::
:::


Aggression $U$ is never observed.

Fairness through unawareness: price on $X$, never on $A$. (The law is more detailed; this is the model's version.)

::: {.source}
Kusner, Loftus, Russell and Silva (2017), "Counterfactual Fairness," *Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems* 30; arXiv:1703.06856v3, Scenario 1, page 4 (with race as $A$). Loftus, *Modern Statistics 4 Data Science*, section 3.4.1, https://joshualoftus.com/ms4ds/ethical-data-science.html#causal-models (with gender).
:::

## Is the price fair? {minutes="2"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: the unaware price, fair to women? Yes or no, one reason.

Two minutes, written, alone.
:::

::: {.ask}
The insurer prices on the car alone. Is that price fair to women? Write yes or no, and one reason.
:::

## The red car in numbers {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: the fit on X alone: who is charged more, who claims more?

Two minutes, in pairs if they like. Answer on the next slide.
:::

$$
X = 2A + U, \qquad Y = U + \varepsilon
$$

$A = 1$ for a woman, half the drivers; $U$ and $\varepsilon$ are $N(0, 1)$, independent of each other and of $A$. Claims and prices are measured from the average.

::: {.ask}
Fit $Y$ on $X$ alone. Which group does it charge more, and by how much? Which group claims more?
:::

## The unaware price

::: {.notes}
Var(X) = 2, Cov(X, Y) = 1, so slope 1/2 and intercept -1/2. The points are a sample; the numbers are population values.
:::

In the population, the price from $X$ alone is $\tfrac12 X - \tfrac12$: women $\tfrac12$ on average, men $-\tfrac12$. Both groups claim $0$.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](slides_files/figure-revealjs/redcar_plot-1.png){fig-alt='Scatter plot of claims Y against car color X for 1,000 simulated drivers, men in navy and women in gold, the gold cloud shifted two units right. A dashed black line of slope one half runs through both clouds; a navy line Y = X and a gold line Y = X - 2 run through the men\'s and the women\'s clouds.' width=100%}
:::
:::


Dashed: the price from $X$ alone. Solid: the best price from $X$ and $A$.

## One driver {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: her price, had she been a man? How?

Let them try before the click. Same U in both columns: the reading's Definition 5.
:::

::: {.ask}
A woman's car scores $X = 2.6$. What would her price have been, had she been a man?
:::

. . .

| | she, $A = 1$ | had $A$ been $0$ |
|:---|---:|---:|
| $U = X - 2A$ | $0.6$ | $0.6$ |
| $X = 2A + U$ | $2.6$ | $0.6$ |
| price from $X$ alone, $\tfrac12 X - \tfrac12$ | $0.8$ | $-0.2$ |
| price from $X$ and $A$, $X - 2A$ | $0.6$ | $0.6$ |

Abduction: find $U$. Action: set $A$. Prediction: recompute with the same $U$.

::: {.source}
Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan (2023), *Fairness and Machine Learning: Limitations and Opportunities*, MIT Press, https://fairmlbook.org/pdf/fairmlbook.pdf, chapter 5, Definition 5, page 123.
:::

## Counterfactual fairness

::: {.notes}
Kusner et al.'s Definition 5 and Lemma 1. Their line on the red car: regressing on X alone "may introduce unfairness into an otherwise fair world."
:::

$\hat Y(a)$: the prediction for the same person, with $U$ as abducted, had $A$ been $a$.

::: {.leave}
$\hat Y$ is counterfactually fair if, given what was observed, $\hat Y(a)$ and $\hat Y(a')$ have the same distribution for every $a'$.
:::

Lemma 1: a function of the non-descendants of $A$ is counterfactually fair. Here, $X - 2A = U$; any non-constant price from $X$ alone is not.

::: {.source}
Kusner, Loftus, Russell and Silva (2017), as above: Definition 5, page 3; Lemma 1, page 5; the quotation, page 4; prices from $X$ alone, page 15.
:::

## Holding aggression fixed {minutes="2"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: what justifies holding U fixed; what evidence for X = 2A + U?

Expected: holding U assumes gender does not shape aggression (no arrow A -> U); 2A + U assumes one additive shift for everyone, and U is never observed, so data on A and X alone cannot check it. Arrows and equations: claims about the world. Which effects count: judgment.

Lens: Leverage. What is held fixed, and is that realistic?
:::

::: {.ask}
The counterfactual driver keeps her $U$. What justifies holding aggression fixed when gender changes? What evidence could support $X = 2A + U$?

Which of your answers are about how the world works vs which effects are acceptable?
:::

## Conditioning or intervening {minutes="2"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: no effect, yet a coefficient of -2: which is conditioning?

Intervening: no directed path from A to Y, so do(A) leaves claims alone. Conditioning: the -2 compares women and men with the same car color. X is a collider of A and U: at a given X, a woman's color owes 2 to A, so her U, and her expected claims, are 2 lower.
:::

::: {.retrieval}
Here $do(A)$ has no effect on $Y$. In the fit $X - 2A$, the coefficient on $A$ is $-2$.

Which is a statement about intervening, and which about conditioning?
:::

# Part 2: Direct and indirect effects

## Mediation example

::: {.notes}
WRITE THE TWO EQUATIONS ON THE BOARD: they are used through Part 3.

Same insurer, a world where gender does affect claims. Men 10,000 miles a year, women 6,000; £25 a year per thousand miles; women £50 less at the same mileage.
:::


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](slides_files/figure-revealjs/dag_mileage-1.png){fig-alt='A causal graph in boxes: A, gender, points into M, mileage, and into Y, claims; M points into Y.' width=100%}
:::
:::


$$
M = \mu_0 + \alpha A + U_M, \qquad Y = \gamma A + \beta M + U_Y
$$

$M$ in thousands of miles a year, $Y$ in hundreds of pounds a year. $U_M$ and $U_Y$ have mean $0$ and are independent of each other and of $A$.

Invented values: $\mu_0 = 10$, $\alpha = -4$, $\beta = 0.25$, $\gamma = -0.5$.

## Splitting an effect {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: for one person, the part of the effect not through mileage?

Most will say: compare at equal mileage. That is the CDE; the natural effect holds mileage at what it would have been.
:::

::: {.ask}
Gender affects claims directly and through mileage. For one person, how would you define the part of the effect that does not go through mileage?
:::

. . .

$Y(a, m)$: claims under $do(A := a, M := m)$. $\quad M(a)$: mileage under $do(A := a)$.

Controlled: $\text{CDE}(m) = E[Y(1, m) - Y(0, m)]$, everyone at mileage $m$.

Natural: $Y(1, M(0))$, a woman driving the miles she would have driven as a man.

::: {.source}
The reading writes $Y_{X := 1,\, Z := Z_{X := 0}}$ for $Y(1, M(0))$, with $X$ the treatment and $Z$ the mediator: Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan (2023), page 128.
:::

## Direct and indirect effects {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
Add and subtract E[Y(1, M(0))]; no model assumed. The reading's indirect effect holds A at 0 (page 128): that pair adds up to the TE only without an interaction.
:::

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{TE} &= E[Y(1, M(1)) - Y(0, M(0))] \\
&= \underbrace{E[Y(1, M(0)) - Y(0, M(0))]}_{\text{NDE}} \\
&\quad + \underbrace{E[Y(1, M(1)) - Y(1, M(0))]}_{\text{NIE}}
\end{aligned}
$$

::: {.leave}
Change gender first, keeping mileage where it would have been; then change mileage, keeping gender at its new value. In any model, the two steps add up to the TE.
:::

## In the linear model {minutes="4"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: CDE, NDE, NIE, TE in the linear model?

CDE: Y(1, m) - Y(0, m) = gamma. NDE: the same at m = M(0), so gamma. NIE: Y(1, M(1)) - Y(1, M(0)) = beta (M(1) - M(0)) = beta (alpha + U_M - U_M) = alpha beta. TE: gamma + alpha beta.
:::

$$
M(a) = \mu_0 + \alpha a + U_M, \qquad Y(a, m) = \gamma a + \beta m + U_Y
$$

::: {.ask}
What are the CDE, the NDE, the NIE and the TE?
:::

. . .

$$
\text{CDE}(m) = \text{NDE} = \gamma, \qquad \text{NIE} = \alpha\beta, \qquad \text{TE} = \gamma + \alpha\beta
$$

::: {.leave}
With no interaction, the indirect effect is the product of the coefficients along the path.
:::

## Holding a mediator fixed {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
Point: the NDE needs Y(1, M(0)), which nobody observes. The tempting substitute, regressing on A and M, holds mileage fixed in the data, not by intervention.
With Z: at equal mileage a woman is more likely to drive for work, so the coefficient on A comes out above gamma, the direct effect.
Lens: Leverage.
:::

$Y(1, M(0))$ is not an observable outcome: the model supplies it.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](slides_files/figure-revealjs/dag_work-1.png){fig-alt='A causal graph in boxes: A, gender, points into M, mileage, and into Y, claims; M points into Y; Z, drives for work, points into M and into Y.' width=100%}
:::
:::


Driving for work, $Z$, adds miles, and adds claims beyond its miles. A woman with the same mileage as a man is more likely to drive for work.

So comparing women and men at equal mileage does not hold mileage fixed: it mixes in $Z$. $M$ is a collider on $A \to M \leftarrow Z$.

# Part 3: Two fair predictors

## Removing the effect of gender {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: from the full premium, remove gender along every path; along A -> Y only.

Two minutes, in pairs; then the hint. Their answers can depend on the parameters.
:::

$$
M = \mu_0 + \alpha A + U_M, \qquad Y = \gamma A + \beta M + U_Y
$$

The insurer's best premium before 2012: $\;Y_{\text{full}} = \gamma A + \beta M$. These premiums are population quantities: they depend on the unknown $\mu_0, \alpha, \beta, \gamma$, and so can your answers.

::: {.ask}
Write down a premium that removes the effect of gender along every path. Then one that removes it along $A \to Y$ only, and keeps the path through mileage.
:::

. . .

Hint: three steps, as for the red car driver. The first is abduction: find $U_M$ from what you observe.

## The counterfactually fair premium {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
Abduction of U_M, then A := 0 along every path; Lemma 1. A woman is priced as if she drove 4,000 more miles.
:::

Abduction: $U_M = M - \mu_0 - \alpha A$. $\;$ Action: $A := 0$. $\;$ Prediction: $M(0) = M - \alpha A$.

$$
Y_{\text{CF}} = \beta\,(M - \alpha A) = \beta\,(\mu_0 + U_M)
$$

A function of $U_M$ alone, so counterfactually fair. It takes $A$ as an input.

## The path-specific premium {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
Chiappa's definition, read out. The direct arrow is judged unfair, the mileage path fair. The result never uses A.
:::

> "a decision is fair toward an individual if it coincides with the one that would have been taken in a counterfactual world in which the sensitive attribute along the unfair pathways were different"

Set $A := 0$ on $A \to Y$ only, and keep the observed mileage:

$$
Y_{\text{PS}} = \beta M
$$

::: {.source}
Chiappa (2019), "Path-Specific Counterfactual Fairness," *Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence* 33(01), 7801--7808, doi:10.1609/aaai.v33i01.33017801, page 7801.
:::

## The premiums compared

::: {.notes}
Each rule shifted by a constant to collect the average claim, £175 a policy; a constant keeps both fairness properties. Gaps: the TE, the NIE, zero. Lens: Ownership.
:::


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](slides_files/figure-revealjs/premiums-1.png){fig-alt='Dot chart of average premiums in hundreds of pounds a year, each rule collecting 1.75 on average. Full prediction: men 2.50, women 1.00. Path-specific: men 2.25, women 1.25. Counterfactually fair: men 1.75, women 1.75.' width=100%}
:::
:::


All three collect the same average premium, £175 a policy: the level is a choice too.

Up to constants: $\;Y_{\text{full}} - Y_{\text{PS}} = \gamma A, \quad Y_{\text{PS}} - Y_{\text{CF}} = \alpha\beta A$

::: {.leave}
The two fair premiums differ by the contribution of the path someone judged fair.
:::

## Your own premium {minutes="2"}

::: {.notes}
QUESTION: which premium for your own policy; and not knowing your group?

Written, alone, one minute; then two answers.
:::

::: {.ask}
Which of the three would you want your own premium computed from? Would you choose the same one if you did not know which group you were in?
:::

## Whose judgment {minutes="3"}

::: {.notes}
Who decides which path is fair. The second quotation is the textbook, not Kusner et al.
:::

Kusner et al., on Berkeley admissions ($A$: female applicant; $X$: course applied to): "the higher rejection among female than male applicants is acceptable, if the mechanism A → X is interpreted as a choice which is under the control of the applicant."

. . .

Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan, on the same example: "This, however, is a substantive judgment that may not be a fact."

. . .

Nabi and Shpitser: "The specific paths which correspond to discrimination are a domain specific issue."

::: {.source}
Kusner, Loftus, Russell and Silva (2017), supplementary material, page 15 of arXiv:1703.06856v3. Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan (2023), chapter 5, page 119. Nabi and Shpitser (2018), "Fair Inference on Outcomes," *Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence* 32(1), doi:10.1609/aaai.v32i1.11553, page 4 of arXiv:1705.10378v4.
:::

## Mediation with an interaction {.inverse minutes="2"}

::: {.notes}
Skip if behind. Why the decomposition pairs its reference levels: here the indirect effect depends on the level of A.
:::

$Y = \gamma A + \beta M + \delta AM + U_Y$: each mile adds $\beta$ for men and $\beta + \delta$ for women. Subscripts give the level held fixed: $\text{NDE}_a$ holds mileage at $M(a)$, $\text{NIE}_a$ holds $A$ at $a$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{NDE}_0 &= \gamma + \delta\mu_0, & \text{NIE}_1 &= \alpha(\beta + \delta) \\
\text{NDE}_1 &= \gamma + \delta(\mu_0 + \alpha), & \text{NIE}_0 &= \alpha\beta
\end{aligned}
$$

$\text{NDE}_0 + \text{NIE}_1 = \text{NDE}_1 + \text{NIE}_0 = \text{TE} = \gamma + \alpha\beta + \delta(\mu_0 + \alpha)$. Mixed pairs miss by $\alpha\delta$: $\text{NDE}_0 + \text{NIE}_0 = \text{TE} - \alpha\delta$. The product $\alpha\beta$ is $\text{NIE}_0$ only.

# Part 4: Luck and responsibility

## Moral luck

::: {.notes}
Moral luck names the problem; the control principle is one response.
:::

> "Moral luck occurs when an agent can be correctly treated as an object of moral judgment despite the fact that a significant aspect of what she is assessed for depends on factors beyond her control." (Nelkin)

. . .

> "The control principle says that we should only judge people to the extent that the factors we judge them on are under their control." (ms4ds)

::: {.source}
Nelkin, "Moral Luck," *The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy*, first published 26 January 2004, substantive revision 20 January 2025, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-luck/, opening sentence. Loftus, *Modern Statistics 4 Data Science*, section 3.4.2, https://joshualoftus.com/ms4ds/ethical-data-science.html#moral-luck.
:::

## Insurance and the middle view {minutes="1"}

::: {.notes}
Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan's middle view of equality of opportunity; insurance is their example.
:::

The middle view of equality of opportunity: decision makers "must, to some degree, treat seemingly dissimilar people similarly when the causes of these dissimilarities are themselves problematic."

> "The middle view has particular purchase in the case of insurance, where we really might want insurers to ignore the additional costs that they are likely to face in setting the price of a policy for someone with an expensive pre-existing condition outside the person's control" (Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan)

::: {.source}
Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan (2023), chapter 4, pages 84 and 85.
